      "text" : "Play D3 and win in South. There are five immediate side-suit tricks, so four clubs suffice. Cash CA, following with C2. If both defenders follow, cash CK with dummy's C7, then lead a low club to CQ. With clubs 3–2, return to South through a major-suit ace and cash the remaining clubs for ten tricks. With clubs 4–1, concede the fourth club round, retain a side-suit stopper to recover the lead, and cash the fifth club for nine tricks. Do not spend the major-suit entries before the clubs are ready. If either defender shows void on CA, stop: neither the automatic duck nor the proposed queen-and-eight sequence guarantees four club tricks against five clubs. Preserve CK, CQ and the intermediate spots while reassessing the actual cards. A possible recovery is a late endplay forcing the long-club defender to lead into the remaining club honors and intermediates, but this requires removing that defender's side-suit exits and retaining suitable entries; it is not assured from the present information. Against accurate defense a 5–0 break may restrict clubs to three tricks and defeat the contract. Prefer the straightforward line that secures the contract against every 3–2 or 4–1 break, without claiming protection against 5–0.",
